WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.089
Lesson two point four.

2
00:00:01.375 --> 00:00:03.473
Splitting Lakh by song, and measuring the

3
00:00:03.473 --> 00:00:04.284
leak that's left.

4
00:00:08.197 --> 00:00:10.563
A model learns from examples, and we need

5
00:00:10.563 --> 00:00:12.767
to know how well it does on music it has

6
00:00:12.767 --> 00:00:13.359
never seen.

7
00:00:13.681 --> 00:00:15.832
So the songs are dealt into three piles

8
00:00:15.832 --> 00:00:17.284
before anything is trained.

9
00:00:17.774 --> 00:00:19.917
The training set: the songs the model

10
00:00:19.917 --> 00:00:20.515
learns from.

11
00:00:20.814 --> 00:00:22.209
Here eighteen of the twenty.

12
00:00:22.508 --> 00:00:24.700
The validation set: songs held back to

13
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.693
choose settings, such as when to stop

14
00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:27.142
training.

15
00:00:27.441 --> 00:00:29.932
And the test set: held back until the end,

16
00:00:29.932 --> 00:00:31.975
and read once, for the score we report.

17
00:00:32.611 --> 00:00:34.691
Now suppose training song r is really

18
00:00:34.691 --> 00:00:37.264
another transcription of test song t: the

19
00:00:37.264 --> 00:00:39.344
same song, typed in by someone else.

20
00:00:39.673 --> 00:00:41.698
Then the model has already heard the

21
00:00:41.698 --> 00:00:42.081
answer.

22
00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:44.819
Its test score measures memory, not music

23
00:00:44.819 --> 00:00:47.173
it has never seen, and it reads too high.

24
00:00:47.501 --> 00:00:48.322
That's leakage.

25
00:00:49.761 --> 00:00:51.027
We've been here before.

26
00:00:51.358 --> 00:00:53.394
In semester one, a random split of

27
00:00:53.394 --> 00:00:55.541
GuitarSet let audio from one recording

28
00:00:55.541 --> 00:00:58.018
session land on both sides, and the team's

29
00:00:58.018 --> 00:01:00.275
random-split runs scored about seventeen

30
00:01:00.275 --> 00:01:02.477
points higher on chords than the player

31
00:01:02.477 --> 00:01:02.807
split.

32
00:01:03.137 --> 00:01:04.844
The fix was to split by player.

33
00:01:05.174 --> 00:01:07.431
This lesson is the same idea, for Lakh.

34
00:01:10.953 --> 00:01:13.363
Lakh was scraped from the web, and popular

35
00:01:13.363 --> 00:01:15.397
songs were typed in by many different

36
00:01:15.397 --> 00:01:15.772
people.

37
00:01:16.093 --> 00:01:17.699
Each person's file is its own

38
00:01:17.699 --> 00:01:20.001
transcription, with its own name and its

39
00:01:20.001 --> 00:01:22.357
own hash, so the same song turns up again

40
00:01:22.357 --> 00:01:22.892
and again.

41
00:01:23.392 --> 00:01:25.627
Lesson two point two's clean-up removed

42
00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:27.917
files that are near copies of each other.

43
00:01:28.252 --> 00:01:30.487
Different people's versions of one song

44
00:01:30.487 --> 00:01:32.107
differ, so they survive it.

45
00:01:33.007 --> 00:01:35.332
The first split dealt each file by its own

46
00:01:35.332 --> 00:01:35.602
hash.

47
00:01:35.926 --> 00:01:38.197
So two transcriptions land in training,

48
00:01:38.197 --> 00:01:40.576
and the third in test: a test song the

49
00:01:40.576 --> 00:01:41.982
model has already learned.

50
00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:43.733
How bad was it?

51
00:01:44.039 --> 00:01:45.924
On the sixth of September we counted.

52
00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:48.318
Of the held-out files that match a known

53
00:01:48.318 --> 00:01:50.458
recording, one thousand one hundred and

54
00:01:50.458 --> 00:01:52.445
thirty-eight out of one thousand seven

55
00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:54.585
hundred and twenty-eight had a sibling in

56
00:01:54.585 --> 00:01:57.031
training: sixty-five point nine percent,

57
00:01:57.031 --> 00:01:57.693
two in three.

58
00:01:58.149 --> 00:02:00.707
And Colin Raffel, who built Lakh, had seen

59
00:02:00.707 --> 00:02:03.320
exactly this: his thesis splits by song,

60
00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:03.973
not by file.

61
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.863
To split by song we first need a rule for

62
00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:11.719
when two files are the same song.

63
00:02:12.057 --> 00:02:13.463
Two files, f i and f j.

64
00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:16.220
The squiggle means: are the same song.

65
00:02:16.557 --> 00:02:18.751
It holds if any one of three things is

66
00:02:18.751 --> 00:02:19.032
true.

67
00:02:19.525 --> 00:02:21.529
One: both are matched to the same

68
00:02:21.529 --> 00:02:23.637
recording in the Million Song Dataset,

69
00:02:23.637 --> 00:02:25.847
with Raffel's match confidence of at least

70
00:02:25.847 --> 00:02:26.207
a half.

71
00:02:26.515 --> 00:02:28.828
Two: the paths the file was found under

72
00:02:28.828 --> 00:02:30.833
name the same artist and title, once

73
00:02:30.833 --> 00:02:32.837
spelling, order and take numbers are

74
00:02:32.837 --> 00:02:35.202
tidied, so Joel comma Billy, The Stranger

75
00:02:35.202 --> 00:02:37.463
two, is the same key as Billy Joel, The

76
00:02:37.463 --> 00:02:37.926
Stranger.

77
00:02:38.234 --> 00:02:40.804
Three: the same title on its own, but only

78
00:02:40.804 --> 00:02:42.757
if it's at least ten letters long and

79
00:02:42.757 --> 00:02:45.070
shared by at most twenty files, so a title

80
00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:47.126
like Yesterday can't swallow the corpus.

81
00:02:48.961 --> 00:02:50.779
Here's why the rule needs care.

82
00:02:51.131 --> 00:02:53.067
File A and file B match the same

83
00:02:53.067 --> 00:02:53.653
recording.

84
00:02:54.005 --> 00:02:56.234
File B and file C share an artist and

85
00:02:56.234 --> 00:02:56.586
title.

86
00:02:56.938 --> 00:02:58.639
A and C share nothing at all.

87
00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:00.458
Union-find joins them.

88
00:03:00.798 --> 00:03:03.181
Every file keeps a pointer to a parent,

89
00:03:03.181 --> 00:03:05.281
and at the start each file points to

90
00:03:05.281 --> 00:03:07.153
itself: three groups of one.

91
00:03:07.494 --> 00:03:09.820
Find follows the pointers up to the file

92
00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:11.579
at the top, the group's root.

93
00:03:12.173 --> 00:03:14.265
Union of A and B finds both roots and

94
00:03:14.265 --> 00:03:16.797
points one at the other: B now points to

95
00:03:16.797 --> 00:03:16.907
A.

96
00:03:17.238 --> 00:03:19.660
Union of B and C finds B's root, which is

97
00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.091
A, so C points to A too.

98
00:03:21.422 --> 00:03:23.734
Now find of C and find of A give the same

99
00:03:23.734 --> 00:03:24.009
root.

100
00:03:24.339 --> 00:03:26.486
One group, even though A and C never

101
00:03:26.486 --> 00:03:27.532
matched each other.

102
00:03:27.992 --> 00:03:29.908
And the whole group is dealt to one side,

103
00:03:29.908 --> 00:03:30.431
never split.

104
00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.958
The groups are put in order by a hash of

105
00:03:35.958 --> 00:03:38.438
their name: a big number that looks random

106
00:03:38.438 --> 00:03:40.763
but is the same every time, so the shuffle

107
00:03:40.763 --> 00:03:41.589
repeats exactly.

108
00:03:41.899 --> 00:03:44.069
Then each group in turn goes to whichever

109
00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:46.497
side is furthest below its target: ninety

110
00:03:46.497 --> 00:03:48.667
percent of the files for training, five

111
00:03:48.667 --> 00:03:50.320
for validation, five for test.

112
00:03:50.726 --> 00:03:52.949
Say training is just over its share and

113
00:03:52.949 --> 00:03:54.782
validation is the furthest under.

114
00:03:55.116 --> 00:03:57.283
Then the next group goes to validation.

115
00:03:57.786 --> 00:03:58.556
One exception.

116
00:03:58.886 --> 00:04:01.031
Covers and medleys chain popular songs

117
00:04:01.031 --> 00:04:03.231
together into one group of six thousand

118
00:04:03.231 --> 00:04:05.706
nine hundred and fifty-six files, thirteen

119
00:04:05.706 --> 00:04:06.916
percent of the corpus.

120
00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:09.171
Any group over one percent goes to

121
00:04:09.171 --> 00:04:09.666
training.

122
00:04:09.996 --> 00:04:12.251
No leak, but validation and test never

123
00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:13.956
contain the most-covered songs.

124
00:04:15.395 --> 00:04:16.283
The final counts.

125
00:04:16.597 --> 00:04:19.002
Corpus version two: forty-seven thousand

126
00:04:19.002 --> 00:04:20.883
five hundred and forty-two songs in

127
00:04:20.883 --> 00:04:23.236
training, and two thousand six hundred and

128
00:04:23.236 --> 00:04:25.222
forty-one each in validation and test.

129
00:04:25.536 --> 00:04:27.940
Version three: forty-seven thousand five

130
00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:30.293
hundred and nineteen, and two thousand six

131
00:04:30.293 --> 00:04:31.861
hundred and thirty-seven each.

132
00:04:32.279 --> 00:04:34.675
Version three was dealt to honour version

133
00:04:34.675 --> 00:04:37.014
two's sides, so the fifty-two thousand

134
00:04:37.014 --> 00:04:39.353
five hundred and fifty-two songs in both

135
00:04:39.353 --> 00:04:41.007
sit on the same side in both.

136
00:04:41.571 --> 00:04:44.278
And the split checks itself: no recording,

137
00:04:44.278 --> 00:04:46.498
no artist and title key, no long title

138
00:04:46.498 --> 00:04:47.256
crosses sides.

139
00:04:47.581 --> 00:04:48.989
But that audit can't fail.

140
00:04:49.314 --> 00:04:51.372
It tests the same three relations the

141
00:04:51.372 --> 00:04:53.592
groups were built from, and groups are

142
00:04:53.592 --> 00:04:54.242
dealt whole.

143
00:04:54.567 --> 00:04:56.895
So zero was guaranteed before any data was

144
00:04:56.895 --> 00:04:57.166
read.

145
00:04:58.686 --> 00:05:01.047
Here's a real pair from version three.

146
00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.595
A validation file found as w slash

147
00:05:03.595 --> 00:05:06.329
wanderer three, and a training file found

148
00:05:06.329 --> 00:05:08.193
as Status Quo, The Wanderer.

149
00:05:08.714 --> 00:05:10.926
Neither matches a recording, and only one

150
00:05:10.926 --> 00:05:11.630
has an artist.

151
00:05:11.931 --> 00:05:14.193
Their titles agree, but wanderer has eight

152
00:05:14.193 --> 00:05:16.154
letters, and a title alone needs ten.

153
00:05:16.455 --> 00:05:17.762
So no relation joins them.

154
00:05:18.064 --> 00:05:20.225
The split put one in validation and one in

155
00:05:20.225 --> 00:05:20.678
training.

156
00:05:20.979 --> 00:05:22.136
Are they the same song?

157
00:05:22.437 --> 00:05:23.644
The split can't tell us.

158
00:05:23.945 --> 00:05:25.302
We need a different signal.

159
00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:30.916
The new signal is the melody, taken from

160
00:05:30.916 --> 00:05:31.491
the guitar.

161
00:05:31.804 --> 00:05:34.103
At each moment a guitar note starts, keep

162
00:05:34.103 --> 00:05:36.402
the highest pitch: that's the top line.

163
00:05:36.715 --> 00:05:38.857
Here are the validation file's first ten

164
00:05:38.857 --> 00:05:40.477
notes, as MIDI pitch numbers.

165
00:05:41.032 --> 00:05:43.281
Now subtract each pitch from the next.

166
00:05:43.636 --> 00:05:46.417
p k is the k-th pitch; d k is the step to

167
00:05:46.417 --> 00:05:48.134
the next one, the interval.

168
00:05:48.489 --> 00:05:51.152
Zero, up seventeen, down fifteen, down

169
00:05:51.152 --> 00:05:52.217
nine, and so on.

170
00:05:52.745 --> 00:05:53.497
Why intervals?

171
00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:56.076
Play the whole song five semitones higher

172
00:05:56.076 --> 00:05:58.063
and every pitch changes, but every

173
00:05:58.063 --> 00:05:59.352
interval stays the same.

174
00:05:59.675 --> 00:06:01.823
So a transcription in another key still

175
00:06:01.823 --> 00:06:02.253
matches.

176
00:06:03.488 --> 00:06:05.913
Take six intervals in a row: a 6-gram.

177
00:06:06.251 --> 00:06:07.886
Each 6-gram is hashed to one

178
00:06:07.886 --> 00:06:09.127
sixty-four-bit number.

179
00:06:09.465 --> 00:06:11.439
This song's first one becomes this.

180
00:06:11.777 --> 00:06:14.202
The same six steps give the same number in

181
00:06:14.202 --> 00:06:14.710
any song.

182
00:06:16.169 --> 00:06:17.733
A whole song gives a lot of these.

183
00:06:18.009 --> 00:06:19.804
The validation file's top line has one

184
00:06:19.804 --> 00:06:21.875
thousand six hundred and forty-five notes,

185
00:06:21.875 --> 00:06:23.347
so one thousand six hundred and

186
00:06:23.347 --> 00:06:25.372
thirty-nine 6-grams, and one thousand and

187
00:06:25.372 --> 00:06:27.166
seventy-five different hash numbers,

188
00:06:27.166 --> 00:06:28.133
because riffs repeat.

189
00:06:28.409 --> 00:06:30.249
Keep only the two hundred and fifty-six

190
00:06:30.249 --> 00:06:30.663
smallest.

191
00:06:30.939 --> 00:06:33.194
That's the sketch, Phi of f: a fixed-size

192
00:06:33.194 --> 00:06:34.483
summary of the whole melody.

193
00:06:34.878 --> 00:06:35.581
Why the smallest?

194
00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:37.691
Every song uses the same hash, so a shared

195
00:06:37.691 --> 00:06:39.139
figure has the same number in both.

196
00:06:39.387 --> 00:06:41.208
If two songs share most of their 6-grams,

197
00:06:41.208 --> 00:06:42.904
their two hundred and fifty-six smallest

198
00:06:42.904 --> 00:06:44.269
numbers are mostly the same ones.

199
00:06:44.517 --> 00:06:46.255
So comparing two small sketches stands in

200
00:06:46.255 --> 00:06:47.496
for comparing the whole songs.

201
00:06:47.744 --> 00:06:49.440
For our pair, the sketches give nought

202
00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.385
point eight three five; all their 6-grams

203
00:06:51.385 --> 00:06:52.874
give nought point eight three three.

204
00:06:53.122 --> 00:06:54.860
Close enough, at a fraction of the cost.

205
00:06:58.097 --> 00:07:00.485
To compare two sketches we use the Jaccard

206
00:07:00.485 --> 00:07:03.207
similarity: the number of things in both,

207
00:07:03.207 --> 00:07:04.651
over the number in either.

208
00:07:04.984 --> 00:07:07.484
One for identical sets, zero for sets with

209
00:07:07.484 --> 00:07:08.483
nothing in common.

210
00:07:08.955 --> 00:07:09.788
Two made-up sets.

211
00:07:10.082 --> 00:07:11.895
A is three, five, eight and nine.

212
00:07:12.189 --> 00:07:14.541
B is five, eight, nine, twelve and twenty.

213
00:07:14.971 --> 00:07:16.624
In both: five, eight and nine.

214
00:07:16.892 --> 00:07:17.160
Three.

215
00:07:17.428 --> 00:07:19.661
In either: three, five, eight, nine,

216
00:07:19.661 --> 00:07:20.421
twelve, twenty.

217
00:07:20.689 --> 00:07:20.867
Six.

218
00:07:21.135 --> 00:07:22.743
So J is three over six: a half.

219
00:07:24.153 --> 00:07:26.383
Dividing by the union matters: a long song

220
00:07:26.383 --> 00:07:28.380
shares more figures by chance, so J asks

221
00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:30.237
what fraction is shared, not how many.

222
00:07:30.516 --> 00:07:31.491
Back to The Wanderer.

223
00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:33.488
Of the two sketches' two hundred and

224
00:07:33.488 --> 00:07:35.253
seventy-nine different numbers, two

225
00:07:35.253 --> 00:07:37.482
hundred and thirty-three are in both: J is

226
00:07:37.482 --> 00:07:38.876
nought point eight three five.

227
00:07:39.154 --> 00:07:41.151
Against an unrelated training song, Long

228
00:07:41.151 --> 00:07:42.545
Tall Sally, they share none.

229
00:07:42.947 --> 00:07:45.148
So a held-out song and its best-matching

230
00:07:45.148 --> 00:07:47.241
training song are called twins if they

231
00:07:47.241 --> 00:07:49.603
share at least thirty-two sketch numbers,

232
00:07:49.603 --> 00:07:51.589
because strangers can share a common

233
00:07:51.589 --> 00:07:54.005
figure like a scale run, and J is at least

234
00:07:54.005 --> 00:07:56.421
a half, so most of the melody is shared,

235
00:07:56.421 --> 00:07:57.440
not a corner of it.

236
00:07:57.763 --> 00:07:59.105
The Wanderer passes both.

237
00:08:02.523 --> 00:08:04.249
Before trusting a detector, test it on

238
00:08:04.249 --> 00:08:05.596
cases where you know the answer.

239
00:08:05.848 --> 00:08:07.826
Four thousand random pairs of songs: none

240
00:08:07.826 --> 00:08:08.710
passes the twin rule.

241
00:08:08.963 --> 00:08:09.636
No false alarms.

242
00:08:09.889 --> 00:08:11.698
Twenty-five twins it found were checked by

243
00:08:11.698 --> 00:08:13.550
hand: twenty-four really were the same

244
00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:15.318
song, things like Hey Joe and Money for

245
00:08:15.318 --> 00:08:15.655
Nothing.

246
00:08:15.907 --> 00:08:17.801
But on pairs we know are one song, because

247
00:08:17.801 --> 00:08:19.569
they match the same recording, it finds

248
00:08:19.569 --> 00:08:21.337
only about fifteen percent, measured at

249
00:08:21.337 --> 00:08:22.431
thirty-two shared numbers.

250
00:08:22.759 --> 00:08:24.640
So the detector is strict but nearly

251
00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:26.877
blind: what it finds is real, and it

252
00:08:26.877 --> 00:08:27.894
misses most of them.

253
00:08:29.368 --> 00:08:31.503
Now search every held-out file against

254
00:08:31.503 --> 00:08:31.996
training.

255
00:08:32.324 --> 00:08:34.350
The grey bars are the old file-level

256
00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:36.485
split, the ochre ones the song-level

257
00:08:36.485 --> 00:08:38.894
split, from looser thresholds to stricter.

258
00:08:39.223 --> 00:08:41.467
With at least eight shared numbers:

259
00:08:41.467 --> 00:08:43.767
thirty-six point seven percent before the

260
00:08:43.767 --> 00:08:45.793
fix, twenty-nine point seven after.

261
00:08:46.121 --> 00:08:48.202
At sixteen, twenty-two then fifteen.

262
00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:51.158
At thirty-two, sixteen then ten: the fix

263
00:08:51.158 --> 00:08:52.472
cut it by about a third.

264
00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:55.428
And at the full twin rule: five point nine

265
00:08:55.428 --> 00:08:57.837
percent, three hundred and twelve of five

266
00:08:57.837 --> 00:08:59.808
thousand two hundred and eighty-two

267
00:08:59.808 --> 00:09:00.630
held-out files.

268
00:09:02.283 --> 00:09:04.588
The detector finds only about one real

269
00:09:04.588 --> 00:09:07.187
twin in seven, so five point nine percent

270
00:09:07.187 --> 00:09:08.192
is a lower bound.

271
00:09:08.546 --> 00:09:11.028
The true leak is higher, by how much we

272
00:09:11.028 --> 00:09:11.619
can't say.

273
00:09:12.121 --> 00:09:14.304
On the September genre-labelled scoring

274
00:09:14.304 --> 00:09:17.032
